### B2.5. Whole-index marks and separation of their phases

For a set \(\mathcal Q\) of marked primes disjoint from the base local set, expand
\(\prod_{q\in\mathcal Q}1_{q\mid A}=\prod_q(1-\chi_q(A)^0)\)
and apply (B2.13). Its simultaneous branch compatibility makes the inactive contribution at \(q\)
\[
1-(1-q_q^{-1})=q_q^{-1}.
\]
The active contribution occurs only in the subtracted zero-mask transform. It contributes \(q_q^{-1/2}\chi_q(\lambda^4\mu)^{-2}\), with scalar \(-\omega_{q,0}\). Thus the marked reflection is
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathcal T_{\mathcal Q}(X;\Psi;V)
&=\sum_{\mathcal B}\zeta_{\mathcal Q}
\sum_{\mu\ne0}\frac{d(\mu)\alpha(\mu)\vartheta(\lambda^4\mu)}{\sqrt{q_\mu}}
\prod_{p\in\mathcal A_0}B_{p,j_p}(\lambda^4\mu)
\prod_{q\in A_{\mathcal Q}}q_q^{-1/2}\chi_q(\lambda^4\mu)^{-2}
V^\sharp(q_\mu X/q_c^2),\\
\zeta_{\mathcal Q}
&=-\frac i{81}\overline{\alpha(c)}^{\,2}
\widehat\varphi(h_0)\overline{\kappa_F}
\prod_{p\in\mathcal I_0}(1-q_p^{-1})\prod_{q\in I_{\mathcal Q}}q_q^{-1}
\prod_{p\in\mathcal A_0}\chi_p(\sigma_p)^{-2}\omega_{p,j_p}
\prod_{q\in A_{\mathcal Q}}\chi_q(\sigma_q)^{-2}(-\omega_{q,0}).
\end{aligned}
\tag{B2.31}
\]
Here \(\mathcal A_0,\mathcal I_0\) are the base active and inactive sets, \(\mathcal Q=A_{\mathcal Q}\sqcup I_{\mathcal Q}\), and \(c=c_F\prod_{p\in\mathcal A_0\cup A_{\mathcal Q}}p\). An inactive marked prime is absent from that branch’s conductor and kernel. If a marked prime belongs to the base local set, its primal term is identically zero and is removed first: its whole-index mark and the base zero extension cannot both be nonzero.

The phase at an active prime has the exact form
\[
\chi_p(\sigma_p)^{-2}\omega_{p,j}
=\xi_{p,j}\chi_p(c/p)^{2j+2},
\qquad |\xi_{p,j}|=1,
\tag{B2.32}
\]
where \(\xi_{p,j}\) depends only on \(p,j\). This follows by inserting \(\epsilon_p=-\lambda^{-5}(c/p)^{-2}\) into (B2.11); for \(j=4\), use \(2j+2\equiv-2\pmod6\). Consequently, two distinct active primes contribute
\[
\chi_p(q)^{2j_p+2}\chi_q(p)^{2j_q+2}
=\left(\frac qp\right)_3^{j_p+j_q+2}.
\tag{B2.33}
\]
A residual row prime has \(j_p=1\), and an active mark has \(j_q=0\). Their phase is therefore one. In contrast, a \(j=4\) prime would leave a nontrivial cubic phase with the residual row, so every nonresidual base prime is fixed before the row norm.

Let \(R\) be the residual squarefree row, \(P\) the product of active marked primes, and \(F_{\rm act}\) the frozen active base product. Fix separate classes of \(R\) and \(P\) modulo the full \(\mathfrak M^2\). This fixes \(d,\vartheta,\kappa_F\) without imposing a pair-dependent sector restriction. Equation (B2.33) assigns row–row phases to a row scalar, mark–mark phases to a tuple scalar, and frozen interactions to the relevant one of these; \(\overline{\alpha(c)}^2\) separates multiplicatively. The removed primal collisions remain as \(1_{(P,R)=1}\).

After fixing the dual unit, ramified power, and extracted frozen factors, the remaining dual part is \(nb^3\). Its moving columns are exactly
\[
\chi_R(nb^3)^3=\chi_R(nb)^3,\qquad
\chi_P(nb^3)^{-2}=\chi_P(n)^{-2}1_{(P,b)=1}.
\tag{B2.34}
\]
The source coefficient, fixed additive character, and frozen local columns depend only on the full extracted dual index. A collision with an extracted frozen prime gives a fixed row, tuple, or dual restriction. Hence after common smooth separation the tuple coefficient is independent of \(R,n,b\), the dual coefficient is independent of \(R,P\), and the remaining mixed masks are exactly those in (B2.29).

